            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The moon primarily symbolizes the Church reflecting the light of Christ (often symbolized by the sun), or serves as an apocalyptic sign of cosmic judgment and a marker of appointed times.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The moon symbolizes the Son (Christ) as the faithful witness who reflects the light of the Father, ruling the night of the world.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"The Church (reflecting Christ's light) or eschatological judgment.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The Son (Christ) reflecting the Father's light.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book reassigns the 'reflected light' metaphor, moving its application from the Church reflecting Christ to Christ reflecting the Father.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus identifies the moon's reflected light with the Church and believers; the book explicitly replaces this common view by identifying the moon as the Son.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book acknowledges the consensus view (the Church reflecting Christ) but replaces it with a new typological framework, equating the moon with the Son by linking the moon's description as a 'faithful witness' (Ps 89:37) to Christ's identical title (Rev 1:5).\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"Psalm 89:37 uses the moon as a simile for the permanence of the Davidic covenant, not necessarily as a typological title for Christ. Furthermore, redefining the moon as Christ disrupts the pervasive biblical and historical symbolism of Christ as the Sun (e.g., Malachi 4:2).\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
